@*
    The flagship case study, as the kit's featured card: the title and summary up
    top, then the argument as one before/after — the problem on the coral side, the
    outcomes on the mint side, joined by the kit's lit rule. A CV bullet has room to
    say what was done; this has room to show how it was decided, which is the part a
    hiring manager is actually assessing.

    The stack reads as mono chips — data, not buttons, so no hover lift. The diagram
    stays behind ArchitectureDiagram's own ghost toggle (Mermaid is 3.5 MB and loads
    only when asked).
*@

<article class="hl-card hl-card--featured case" id="@Study.Slug" data-hl-reveal>
    <header class="case__head">
        <h3 class="hl-h3 case__title">@Study.Title</h3>
        @*
            Attribution reads as a byline under the title, never as a label above it:
            the heading carries its own weight, and the organisation is metadata.
        *@
        <p class="case__org">@(Study.Organisation ?? "Personal project")</p>
        <p class="hl-body case__summary">@Study.Summary</p>
    </header>

    <div class="hl-compare">
        <div class="hl-compare__from">
            <h4 class="hl-h4">Problem</h4>
            <p class="hl-body">@Study.Problem</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hl-compare__to">
            <h4 class="hl-h4">Outcome</h4>
            <ul class="outcomes">
                @foreach (var outcome in Study.Outcomes)
                {
                    <li>@outcome</li>
                }
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    @if (Study.Evidence.Count > 0)
    {
        <p class="evidence-line">
            <span class="evidence-line__label">Verify</span>
            @foreach (var (link, i) in Study.Evidence.Select((link, i) => (link, i)))
            {
                if (i > 0)
                {
                    <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>
                }
                <a href="@link.Url" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@link.Label</a>
            }
        </p>
    }

    @if (Study.Stack.Count > 0)
    {
        <ul class="case__stack">
            @foreach (var item in Study.Stack)
            {
                <li class="hl-chip case__chip">@item</li>
            }
        </ul>
    }

    <div class="case__actions">
        <a class="hl-btn hl-btn--primary hl-btn--sm" href="@($"/work/{Study.Slug}")">Read the case study</a>

        @*
            The write-up button renders only when it goes somewhere the case-study
            button does not: the panel once showed both as two pills to the same URL,
            which reads as padding on the one study whose claims most need a second
            destination (an external repo or essay) to be believed.
        *@
        @if (Study.HasWriteup && Study.WriteupUrl != $"/work/{Study.Slug}")
        {
            <a class="hl-btn hl-btn--ghost hl-btn--sm" href="@Study.WriteupUrl">Full technical write-up</a>
        }

        @if (Study.HasDiagram)
        {
            <ArchitectureDiagram Id="@($"panel-{Study.Slug}")" Source="@Study.MermaidDiagram" />
        }
    </div>
</article>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required CaseStudy Study { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public int Index { get; set; }
}
